{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of Baldwin II King of Jerusalem and Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. He was around 12 years old at the time and was still a minor. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174. She was the daughter of Walter of Bures Prince of Galilee and Eschiva of Saint-Omer. The marriage was likely arranged by King Amalric I of Jerusalem who wanted to strengthen his alliance with the Count of Tripoli.\n\n*Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nRaymond III had a strained relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a powerful and independent-minded count who often clashed with the kings of Jerusalem. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din Zengi the ruler of Aleppo while on a military campaign. He was held captive for several years and was released in 1173.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the leaders of the Crusader army and fought alongside King Guy of Lusignan. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond III was forced to flee. He retreated to Tripoli and began to rebuild his forces.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. Raymond III was remembered as a brave and chivalrous knight who fought tirelessly for 